OPPS CONCEPTS:
1. Encapsulation:
Encapsulation means wrapping data (variables) and methods into a single unit (class).
- Use private variables.
- Access them using getters and setters.
- Provides data protection.
Real-time example: ATM machine. You cannot directly access the bank's internal data.

ex:class Bank {
    private int balance = 5000;

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }

    public void setBalance(int amount) {
        balance = amount;
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Bank b = new Bank();
        System.out.println(b.getBalance());
    }
}

2. Inheritance:
Inheritance means one class acquiring the properties and methods of another class.
- Parent class (Superclass)
- Child class (Subclass)
- Uses extends keyword.
Real-time example: A child inherits certain characteristics from parents.

ex:class Animal {
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Dog is barking");
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog d = new Dog();
        d.eat();
        d.bark();
    }
}


3.Polymorphism:
Polymorphism means one name having many forms.
There are two types:
A. Compile-time Polymorphism (Method Overloading)
name method name but different parameters.

ex:class Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Calculator c = new Calculator();

        System.out.println(c.add(10, 20));
     System.out.println(c.add(10, 20, 30));
    }
}

B. Runtime Polymorphism (Method Overriding)

A child class provides its own implementation of a parent class method.


ex:class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal a = new Dog();
        a.sound();
    }
}


4. Abstraction:
Abstraction means hiding implementation details and showing only essential information.

There are two ways to achieve abstraction in Java:
- Abstract class
- Interface
Real-time example: When you drive a car, you use the steering wheel and brakes without knowing the complete internal mechanism.

Abstract class example

ex:abstract class Vehicle {
    abstract void start();
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    void start() {
        System.out.println("Car starts with a key");
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car c = new Car();
        c.start();
    }
}


